The release packages additionally bundle two programs that are licensed under
the GNU General Public License:

  * mpv      https://mpv.io                  GPLv2 or later
  * FFmpeg   https://ffmpeg.org  (ffmpeg, ffprobe)  GPLv2 or later

These are separate programs. PlayerOne runs them as child processes and links
no GPL code into its own executable, which is why PlayerOne's source can carry
the MIT licence while those binaries keep theirs.

If you redistribute a package containing those binaries, their GPL obligations
travel with them: recipients must be able to obtain the corresponding source.
The binaries come from published upstream builds, and pointing at those sources
is the practical way to satisfy this:

  * mpv Windows builds    https://github.com/shinchiro/mpv-winbuild-cmake
  * mpv source            https://github.com/mpv-player/mpv
  * FFmpeg Windows builds https://github.com/BtbN/FFmpeg-Builds
  * FFmpeg source         https://github.com/FFmpeg/FFmpeg

scripts/fetch-tools.ps1 downloads exactly these binaries and prints their
versions, so a release always corresponds to a published upstream build.

Full detail, including how to build and distribute PlayerOne without bundling
them at all, is in docs/third-party.md.
